1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:08,212
1977. The first general manager of a small computer company hands out employee numbers.

2
00:00:08,412 --> 00:00:13,412
Number one goes to the engineer who designed the computer. Number two, to his partner.

3
00:00:13,612 --> 00:00:19,458
The partner gets angry. He wants to be number one. As it was told later, he even cries.

4
00:00:19,658 --> 00:00:29,427
The manager, Mike Scott, gives him zero: before number one. But the payroll bank wouldn't accept zero, so on paper he remained number two.

5
00:00:30,377 --> 00:00:32,838
The partner's name was Steve Jobs.

6
00:00:33,388 --> 00:00:41,542
Today that company is worth around five trillion dollars. This is the story of Apple, told through its strangest anecdotes.

7
00:00:42,392 --> 00:00:45,777
CHAPTER 1 — A CALL TO THE VATICAN

8
00:00:46,127 --> 00:00:57,435
It all begins with two Steves: Wozniak, an electronics genius, and Jobs, five years younger. In 1971 they read something incredible in a magazine.

9
00:00:57,885 --> 00:01:03,808
Let's go to the chalkboard. Back then, telephone exchanges talked to each other using sounds.

10
00:01:04,008 --> 00:01:13,623
A 2600 Hz tone meant: free line. And a toy whistle, a free prize inside a cereal box, made that exact tone.

11
00:01:13,823 --> 00:01:21,131
If you blew it into the phone, the exchange handed you the line. And you could call anywhere in the world, for free.

12
00:01:22,081 --> 00:01:26,850
The largest telephone network in the world was fooled by a cereal toy.

13
00:01:27,050 --> 00:01:37,204
Wozniak built a little blue box that made those tones. One day he called the Vatican and, with a fake accent, claimed to be Henry Kissinger. He asked to speak to the Pope.

14
00:01:37,404 --> 00:01:44,404
According to Jobs, they actually sent someone to wake up the Pope. Then they burst out laughing, and were caught.

15
00:01:45,354 --> 00:01:53,969
Jobs sold about two hundred boxes. One built them and the other sold them. Apple already existed, even if it didn't have a name yet.

16
00:01:54,819 --> 00:01:58,204
CHAPTER 2 — TWO STEVES, ONE PAYCHECK

17
00:01:58,554 --> 00:02:05,015
In 1974, Jobs joined Atari, the hottest video game company around, as a technician.

18
00:02:05,215 --> 00:02:13,369
He had a theory: since he ate almost only fruit, he didn't need to shower often. His coworkers did not agree.

19
00:02:13,569 --> 00:02:24,723
Atari moved him to the night shift. Its founder, Nolan Bushnell, later said he got a bonus out of it: Wozniak came in at night to help him. Two Steves for the price of one.

20
00:02:24,923 --> 00:02:32,846
Atari asked Jobs for a game using very few chips: Breakout. Wozniak designed it in four nights, barely sleeping.

21
00:02:33,046 --> 00:02:41,508
Jobs told him they had been paid 700 dollars, and gave him half. According to Wozniak, the real bonus was around 5,000.

22
00:02:42,458 --> 00:02:51,227
He found out about ten years later, reading a book. He said he cried. And that if Jobs had just asked for the money, he would have given it to him.

23
00:02:52,077 --> 00:02:54,769
CHAPTER 3 — APRIL FOOLS' DAY

24
00:02:55,119 --> 00:03:01,658
Apple was founded on April 1, 1976. In the United States, April Fools' Day.

25
00:03:01,858 --> 00:03:12,473
There were three partners. The third, Ronald Wayne, 41, held 10%. He drew the first logo: Isaac Newton reading under an apple tree.

26
00:03:12,673 --> 00:03:20,519
Twelve days later, frightened by potential debts, he sold his share for 800 dollars. He always said he didn't regret it.

27
00:03:20,719 --> 00:03:28,796
A local store had ordered 50 computers. To buy the parts, Jobs sold his van and Wozniak sold his calculator.

28
00:03:28,996 --> 00:03:36,765
The retail price: 666.66 dollars. More than a few saw the number of the beast.

29
00:03:37,715 --> 00:03:42,408
There was nothing satanic about it. Wozniak simply liked repeating digits.

30
00:03:43,258 --> 00:03:46,950
CHAPTER 4 — THE COMPUTER THAT DIDN'T EXIST

31
00:03:47,300 --> 00:03:54,454
In 1977, at a major computer fair in San Francisco, Apple unveiled the Apple II.

32
00:03:54,654 --> 00:04:06,654
And Wozniak, in secret, handed out thousands of brochures for a spectacular machine: the Zaltair. It didn't exist. It was a parody of the Altair.

33
00:04:06,854 --> 00:04:16,085
Jobs fell for it. He checked the comparison chart and felt proud: the Apple II held its own. Wozniak told him the truth years later.

34
00:04:16,285 --> 00:04:27,746
Other surprises weren't on purpose. In 1980 came the Apple III, with no fan, as Jobs wanted. From the heat, chips popped out of their sockets.

35
00:04:27,946 --> 00:04:37,023
According to accounts from that era, some customers were advised to lift it a few inches and drop it. That pushed the chips back into place.

36
00:04:37,973 --> 00:04:42,204
Tech support fit into a single word: drop it.

37
00:04:43,054 --> 00:04:45,977
CHAPTER 5 — BETTER TO BE A PIRATE

38
00:04:46,327 --> 00:04:53,942
To keep Apple from turning bureaucratic, Jobs repeated a line: it's better to be a pirate than to join the navy.

39
00:04:54,142 --> 00:05:03,373
The Macintosh team sewed a pirate flag and raised it on the roof. The rival team, building the Lisa computer, stole it and demanded a ransom.

40
00:05:03,573 --> 00:05:15,342
At its unveiling in 1984, the Macintosh spoke on its own. And it mocked giant computers: never trust a computer you can't lift.

41
00:05:15,792 --> 00:05:23,408
Who drew what appeared on that screen? Susan Kare. Let's go to the chalkboard, because this hits close to home for this channel.

42
00:05:23,608 --> 00:05:32,685
The screen was a grid of tiny dots, black or white. One icon took up 32 by 32: 1,024 little squares.

43
00:05:32,885 --> 00:05:41,885
Kare didn't have a computer to design on. She bought a $2.50 graph paper notebook and colored every icon, square by square.

44
00:05:42,835 --> 00:05:48,296
The most famous icons of their era were pixel art. And they were drawn with a pencil.

45
00:05:49,146 --> 00:05:51,992
CHAPTER 6 — SO SUE ME

46
00:05:52,342 --> 00:06:02,804
In 1985, after a clash with the board, Jobs left his own company. He founded another, NeXT, and paid $100,000 for the logo.

47
00:06:03,004 --> 00:06:11,773
Apple had another problem: the Beatles' company was also called Apple. To avoid lawsuits, it promised never to enter the music business.

48
00:06:11,973 --> 00:06:21,896
But computers were making more and more sounds. In 1989 came another lawsuit, and lawyers vetted even the names of system sounds.

49
00:06:22,096 --> 00:06:33,558
Engineer Jim Reekes was told one of his sound names wouldn't pass legal review. He replied: 'so sue me.' And he spelled the sound Sosumi.

50
00:06:34,508 --> 00:06:41,508
The lawyers approved it without realizing. More than thirty years later, that joke is still chiming on Macs.

51
00:06:42,358 --> 00:06:45,127
CHAPTER 7 — ONE DOLLAR A YEAR

52
00:06:45,477 --> 00:06:51,862
Without Jobs, Apple did worse and worse. By the mid-nineties it stood on the brink of bankruptcy.

53
00:06:52,062 --> 00:06:59,908
It bought NeXT to get an operating system, and with the company came Jobs. He asked for a salary of one dollar a year.

54
00:07:00,108 --> 00:07:12,492
In 1997 he announced that Microsoft, the arch-rival, was investing $150 million in Apple. Bill Gates appeared on a giant screen, and the audience booed.

55
00:07:12,692 --> 00:07:17,538
Jobs also had his quirks. His silver sports car never had a license plate.

56
00:07:17,738 --> 00:07:27,508
According to a former Apple security chief, the law gave a grace period to put plates on a new car. Jobs leased an identical one every six months.

57
00:07:28,458 --> 00:07:37,842
California traffic authorities said it didn't quite work that way. What's certain is that he had saved Apple. And that the plate never showed up.

58
00:07:38,692 --> 00:07:41,615
CHAPTER 8 — FOUR THOUSAND LATTES

59
00:07:41,965 --> 00:07:49,504
January 2007. Jobs unveils the iPhone. The audience had no idea the prototype barely worked.

60
00:07:49,704 --> 00:07:59,781
It crashed and dropped signal, so the signal bars were hardcoded to always show five. And in the fifth row, engineers passed around a flask of whisky.

61
00:08:00,231 --> 00:08:04,538
Let's go to the chalkboard. Picture the phone's memory as a glass.

62
00:08:04,738 --> 00:08:12,969
Every open app pours water into it. Unfinished apps didn't give the water back, and when the glass overflowed, the phone froze.

63
00:08:13,169 --> 00:08:22,631
If you sent an email and then browsed, it worked. The other way around, it crashed. So they mapped out one exact sequence: the golden path.

64
00:08:23,581 --> 00:08:27,427
The iPhone worked, as long as nobody stepped off the script.

65
00:08:27,627 --> 00:08:36,781
To demo maps, Jobs called a real Starbucks live on stage. He ordered 4,000 lattes to go, and hung up: wrong number.

66
00:08:37,731 --> 00:08:43,423
The first public phone call ever made on an iPhone was a prank call. Just like in the Vatican days.

67
00:08:44,273 --> 00:08:47,119
CHAPTER 9 — PIRATE OR NAVY?

68
00:08:47,469 --> 00:08:54,546
Jobs passed away in 2011, at 56. Tim Cook led Apple for the next fifteen years.

69
00:08:54,746 --> 00:09:03,977
For Apple's 50th anniversary in 2026, Paul McCartney played at its headquarters. A Beatle, after nearly thirty years of lawsuits over the name.

70
00:09:04,177 --> 00:09:11,485
That year a new chief took over, engineer John Ternus. And Apple launched its first foldable iPhone.

71
00:09:11,685 --> 00:09:19,146
Today it's worth around five trillion dollars, battling NVIDIA for the title of most valuable company on Earth.

72
00:09:19,346 --> 00:09:25,423
Jobs preferred pirates. But Apple is now one of the largest navies on the planet.

73
00:09:26,373 --> 00:09:29,912
The question is whether it still has any pirate left in it.

74
00:09:30,862 --> 00:09:37,323
What do you think? What story would you like to see in the next episode? Let us know down in the comments.
